  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Alabama totaled $10.8B in FY2026 to date, spread across 77,366 awards with an average award size of $140K. Obligations fell 40.3% year over year, and the current-year total is a partial, fiscal year to date figure. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share at $7.9B, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $1.6B. Contracting in the state is heavily weighted toward aerospace, defense, and R&D: the top industry is Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $3.2B, and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $1.8B. Large prime contractors active in Alabama include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $1.1B, THE BOEING COMPANY at $1B, and DYNETICS, INC. at $1B. Ship building, engineering services, and a mix of software and custom programming work also appear in the top industry list, showing a blend of platform manufacturing and technical services driving obligations in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Alabama?",
        "body": "Buying is concentrated: the Department of Defense accounts for $7.9B of the $10.8B total. The next-largest buyer is the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $1.6B, with all other agencies considerably smaller in scale."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What types of work dominate Alabama contract obligations?",
        "body": "Work in Alabama is centered on research, development, and aerospace manufacturing: Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is $3.2B and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is $1.8B. Engineering Services, Ship Building and Repairing, and several software and computer programming categories also rank among the top industries."
      }
    ]
  },
